    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_03=§7§m-----------------------------------------
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_04=Initial pH value every cycle varies from §c4.5§7 - §c9.5 pH
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_05=If the pH value is within §c0.05 pH §7of 7.0 pH at the end of the cycle, the recipe always succeeds
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_06=Otherwise, the recipe always fails
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_07=Use a pH Sensor Hatch to read the current pH value
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_08=For safety, the machine will shut down if the pH goes below 0 or exceeds 14
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_09=§7§m-----------------------------------------
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_10=Every §c20§7 ticks, consumes ALL §fSodium Hydroxide§7 and §fHydrochloric Acid§7 in the special hatches
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_11=§cRaises §7the pH value by §c0.01 pH §7per piece of §fSodium Hydroxide Dust
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_12=§cLowers §7the pH value by §c0.01 pH §7per §c10L §7of §fHydrochloric Acid
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_13=§7§m-----------------------------------------
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_14=§b§oThe fourth step of water purification is to neutralize the solution and bring its pH to exactly 7, rendering
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_15=§b§othe solution inert with no hydrogen ion activity beyond water’s natural amphiproticity. Acids and bases from soils
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_16=§b§oand geology cause natural alkalinity variations in water which can cause corrosive reactions with sensitive
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_17=§b§omaterials. This necessitates the use of the corresponding neutralizing agents to pH balance the water.
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_18=§7§m-----------------------------------------
    S:TileEntity_DESCRIPTION_09406_Index_19=Hold §l[LSHIFT]§r§7 to display structure guidelines
